{"kind":"artifact","text":"Merged the approved resident append cache to main at bbea2560665a39b10b21b4691e856e82fdcbff1c. This merges task/resident-append at exactly 9c4f591545ee54e1877ab8cce0d3579a15226ab7, the head I approved in bfbde3ce and which codex ratified as the review requester. A fast-forward was available, so although the merge carries its own commit the resulting tree is byte-identical to the approved head, which I verified rather than assumed: nothing entered main that was not reviewed. make vet, make test, go test -race ./..., and make build all pass on merged main. What lands is the resident sequencer retaining only a log state that scanHead has fully verified and reusing it only while the target ref still names that exact head, so warm append cost stops growing with the log — flat at roughly 73 to 76 milliseconds and about 850 allocations per append whether the log holds ten events or forty, against a stateless path that rises from 342 to 1,118 milliseconds and from 3,675 to 11,703 allocations over the same span. Cold start, external ref advance, CAS loss, target change, and the stateless path all still pay for a full scan. The reason I was willing to approve a cache on the correctness-critical path is that the trust boundary was checked as carefully as the speed claim: caller-owned signed bytes are cloned before caching, and the decoded intent, which is cached uncloned and carries a byte-slice capability hash, was probed through this repository's own CBOR decode mode and version to confirm it returns no window into the caller's buffer. Serialization was exercised under contention with thirty-six concurrent submissions through one submitter under the race detector, where duplicates collapsed to single commits, an independent verification agreed with the cached head, and a genuine idempotency conflict was still refused. Two known non-blocking properties travel with this merge: the submitter mutex is held across the whole submission including git subprocess calls and the PreAppend hook, so resident throughput is serialized and a re-entrant hook would deadlock, and the benchmark only demonstrates its claim when run with enough iterations, since at a single iteration the resident and stateless paths are indistinguishable.","body":{"commit":"bbea2560665a39b10b21b4691e856e82fdcbff1c","path":"."}}